  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high that quiet and dry weather will persist\ntoday and tomorrow as high pressure remains over the Great \nLakes and Ohio River Valley. Temperatures will remain near to \nslightly above climatological averages, supported by deep \nnorthwesterly flow on the backside of a trough. \n\nLong-range models depict a pattern change Wednesday into\nThursday as low pressure develops and strengthens over the\nPacific Northwest and tracks eastward into southern Canada and \nthe Great Lakes. This system will weaken the ridge over the \ncentral CONUS and allow more active weather to develop across \nthe region. \n\nTemperatures will trend above average Wednesday as southwest\nflow returns, with dew points rising into the mid to upper-60s\nand resulting in increasingly muggy conditions. Heat index\nvalues could approach 90 degrees by late Wednesday afternoon in\na few areas. By late Wednesday into early Thursday morning,\nprecipitation chances will increase as a trough and associated \ncold front moves across the Great Lakes. The probability of \nstrong to severe storms is currently around 5-15%. \n\nThe prolonged passage of the trough may support periods of\nmoderate to heavy rainfall totals on Thursday, potentially\nresulting in isolated flooding near creeks and streams. Some \nareas could receive 1 to 1.5 inches of rainfall during this \nperiod. \n\nDrier conditions are expected Friday with the exit of the cold\nfront and a building ridge.",
